# ─── Open Harness — the configuration file ───────────────────────────
# Copy to the gitignored .devcontainer/.env before building:
#
#     cp .devcontainer/.example.env .devcontainer/.env
#
# `oh init` does this for you and fills in host defaults; `oh harness install <name>` and `oh sandbox` edit single keys.
#
# THIS IS THE ONLY CONFIGURATION SURFACE, and it works on EVERY path:
#   - `oh sandbox` (and `make ...` in the source repo) goes through
#     .oh/scripts/docker-compose.sh, which passes this file with --env-file;
#   - the VS Code "Reopen in Container" path loads .devcontainer/docker-compose.yml
#     directly, and compose auto-loads .devcontainer/.env from the same directory.
# harness.yaml, which used to sit in front of this file, was removed in 0.4.0
# precisely because it was invisible on the second path.
#
# FORMAT: `KEY=value`, one per line, no `export`. A `#` at the start of a line
# comments it out; a `#` INSIDE a value is part of the value, not a comment.
# Quote a value only if you also want to `source` this file from a shell.
#
# PRECEDENCE: a variable already exported in the shell that runs `oh`
# beats the value here. Every key below also has a default baked into
# .devcontainer/docker-compose.yml, so a key left commented is not "unset" — it
# takes that default. Each default is shown.
#
# Apply changes with:  oh stop && oh sandbox

# ─── Sandbox identity ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SANDBOX_NAME=openharness              # container + compose project name
# TZ=America/Los_Angeles                # cron schedules, log timestamps

# ─── Project root seam (RFC #531 Phase 1) ────────────────────────────
# The container path the repo is mounted at. Leave unset unless relocating.
# OH_PROJECT_ROOT=/home/sandbox/harness

# ─── Prebuilt image (instead of a local build) ───────────────────────
# Run a published image rather than building from .devcontainer/Dockerfile.
# Pair with `oh sandbox --image`. Recipe: docs/deployment-prebuilt-image.md.
# OH_SANDBOX_IMAGE=ghcr.io/mifunedev/openharness:latest
# OH_PULL_POLICY=missing                # compose pull policy: missing|always|never

# ─── Git identity inside the sandbox ─────────────────────────────────
# GIT_USER_NAME=Your Name               # spaces are fine, no quotes needed
# GIT_USER_EMAIL=you@example.com

# ─── Optional installs (off by default) ──────────────────────────────
# `oh harness install <name>` writes these keys for you — it uncomments the
# line in place AND installs into the running sandbox, no rebuild. The four
# harness keys map to `oh harness` names: opencode, grok-build, deepagents,
# hermes. `agent_browser` is not a harness — `oh tool install agent-browser`
# manages that one.
# INSTALL_OPENCODE=false                # OpenCode CLI (build arg)
# INSTALL_GROK_BUILD=false              # Grok Build CLI (build arg)
# INSTALL_DEEPAGENTS=false              # DeepAgents CLI (build arg)
# INSTALL_HERMES=false                  # Hermes CLI (build arg + runtime)
# INSTALL_AGENT_BROWSER=false           # agent-browser + Chromium (~1 GB)

# ─── Hermes dashboard ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Applies the docker-compose.hermes-dashboard.yml overlay when truthy.
# HERMES_DASHBOARD=false                # auto-start the web dashboard
# HERMES_DASHBOARD_PORT=9119            # host loopback port for the dashboard

# ─── Host Docker socket (off by default — security-sensitive) ────────
# Applies the docker-compose.docker-sock.yml overlay when truthy. Mounting
# /var/run/docker.sock is effectively HOST ROOT: an agent can start a
# privileged container that mounts the host filesystem. See
# docs/security-considerations.md.
# DOCKER_SOCKET=false

# ─── Sandbox user password (login + sudo) ────────────────────────────
# The `sandbox` user's password, used for BOTH remote login (SSH/`su sandbox`)
# AND sudo. Sudo is NOT passwordless (/etc/sudoers.d/sandbox is
# `sandbox ALL=(ALL) ALL`): an interactive operator who knows this password can
# escalate, but a non-interactive internal agent hits the password prompt and
# fails (`sudo -n` returns "a password is required"). With the Docker socket
# mounted the sandbox user is also in the `docker` group, which remains an
# independent root-escalation path — this gates `sudo`, not all escalation.
# SECURITY: the default is weak and known; override on any network-reachable
# deployment, from here or your platform's secret store.
# SANDBOX_PASSWORD=test1234

# ─── Direct SSH into the container (opt-in) ──────────────────────────
# Applies the docker-compose.ssh.yml overlay when truthy. See
# docs/integrations/sshd.md.
# SANDBOX_SSH=false                     # run sshd for direct container SSH
# SANDBOX_SSH_PORT=2222                 # host loopback port published for SSH
#
# Auth is PUBLIC-KEY by default: paste one or more public keys below (newline or
# literal \n separated). This is public key material, not a secret.
# Without a key AND without password auth, no one can log in — sshd warns loudly.
# SANDBOX_SSH_AUTHORIZED_KEYS="ssh-ed25519 AAAA... you@laptop"
#
# Password auth (uses SANDBOX_PASSWORD above) is OFF unless you flip it on.
# Never enable it on a public-facing bind while SANDBOX_PASSWORD is the default.
# SANDBOX_SSH_PASSWORD_AUTH=false

# ─── Cron runtime ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CRONS_DIR=.oh/crons                   # directory the cron runtime scans
# CRON_AGENT_BIN=claude                 # binary that fires scheduled tasks

# ─── Harness data directories (resolved by .oh/scripts/oh-path) ──────
# WORKTREES_DIR=.worktrees              # git worktree root (this repo)
# PROJECTS_DIR=projects                 # non-harness project-clone root

# ─── Build behaviour ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SKIP_PNPM_INSTALL=0                   # 1 skips the entrypoint's pnpm install

# ─── Compose overlays ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Extra `-f` overlay paths are a LIST, which this format cannot hold. They live
# in .oh/config.json under composeOverrides[], applied after the built-in
# overlays above (last -f wins).

# ─── GitHub (primary git auth method) ────────────────────────────────
# Personal Access Token for GitHub CLI authentication inside the sandbox.
# On startup, entrypoint auto-runs `gh auth login`, `gh auth setup-git`,
# and (if `admin:public_key` scope is granted) generates an ed25519 SSH
# key and uploads the public key to GitHub — no interactive prompts.
# Recommended: Set this for frictionless git operations and gh CLI automation.
# Create one at: https://github.com/settings/tokens?type=beta
# Scopes needed: repo, read:org, admin:public_key (for SSH key upload)
GH_TOKEN=

# ─── xAI / Grok Build ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional API key fallback for the Grok Build CLI. The preferred browser or
# device auth flow writes persistent state under ~/.grok (Docker volume), but
# this secret can be passed through for non-interactive use.
# XAI_API_KEY=

# ─── Optional Langfuse observability for Pi only ──────────────────────
# pi-langfuse reads these only from the environment of the Pi process. This
# file provides Docker Compose interpolation; it is not automatically
# injected wholesale into Pi. Claude Code uses its Langfuse marketplace plugin's
# `/plugin configure` flow instead; do not add Claude plugin credentials here.
# If using these Pi values, launch Pi from a shell with:
#     set -a; source /home/sandbox/harness/.devcontainer/.env; set +a; pi
# See docs/integrations/langfuse.md for Pi precedence and privacy guidance.
# LANGFUSE_PUBLIC_KEY=pk-lf-...
# LANGFUSE_SECRET_KEY=sk-lf-...
# LANGFUSE_BASE_URL=http://langfuse-web:3000
# LANGFUSE_PRIVACY_PRESET=metadata-only

# ─── Slack bot (pi-messenger-bridge) ─────────────────────────────────
# The pi-messenger-bridge package bridges Slack messages into a Pi agent
# over Socket Mode. Read from process.env at session_start; no compose
# overlay needed.
#
# Load into the shell that launches `pi` inside the sandbox:
#     set -a; source /home/sandbox/harness/.devcontainer/.env; set +a
# `set -a` is required because this file uses Compose's KEY=value
# format (no `export`); without it, sourced vars stay in the shell
# and aren't inherited by child processes like `pi`.
#
# Runtime config (autoConnect + challenge-auth trustedUsers) lives in
# the tracked ~/.pi/msg-bridge.json (hand-edited; the entrypoint copies it
# into ~/.pi/ on boot). There is no static allowlist — access is gated by
# runtime challenge-auth.
# See docs/integrations/slack.md.

# Slack app-level token for Socket Mode connection (starts with xapp-).
# PI_SLACK_APP_TOKEN=xapp-...

# Slack bot OAuth token for posting messages (starts with xoxb-).
# PI_SLACK_BOT_TOKEN=xoxb-...
